Function: ref_pp_setup_dir1
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 54-88, 93-114, 155-174
Inputs:
Outputs: x, y, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt, T, alpha,
 beta, gamma, delta, sigma_x, sigma_y
Pairs: aci_predprey_model, aci_predprey_components
Note: Common model setup, direction-one components, and direction-one signal
 generation, including the reference's own rng(42). Three ranges rather than
 one because the script interleaves its two causal directions; taking them in
 file order is exactly what a user does following its "RUN THIS CODE SECTION"
 instructions. N = 12000 is fixed inside the range, so this reproduces the
 PUBLISHED dataset -- which is what allows the committed fixtures to be
 re-graded at full scale, the filter and smoother both being O(N).

Function: ref_pp_setup_dir2
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 54-88, 122-143, 182-201
Inputs:
Outputs: x, y, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt, T, alpha,
 beta, gamma, delta, sigma_x, sigma_y
Pairs: aci_predprey_model, aci_predprey_components
Note: The direction-two mirror. Shares the common setup range with
 ref_pp_setup_dir1, so both see the same rng(42) and the same realisation.

Function: ref_enso_setup_and_signals
Script: ENSO_model_cond_ACI_u_h_W_tau_unobs.m
Lines: 880-1163
Inputs:
Outputs: u, h_W, T_C, T_E, tau, I, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, S_x, S_y, N, dt, T, k_dt
Pairs: aci_enso_parameters, aci_enso_components, aci_enso_model
Note: Model setup and simulation for the configuration in which (T_C, T_E, I)
 are observed and (u, h_W, tau) are not. Every coefficient is matrix-valued and
 seasonally modulated, and BOTH noise covariances vary in time -- the only
 system in the reference for which that is true, and the reason aciR's
 validators must invert every slice rather than the first. The simulation is
 the reference's own, announced by its progress bar as a Milstein scheme; see
 design/2026-08-13_milstein_anomaly.md for the open question about the
 correction term it applies.

Function: ref_enso_filter
Script: ENSO_model_cond_ACI_u_h_W_tau_unobs.m
Lines: 1209-1272
Inputs: T_C, T_E, I, u, h_W, tau, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, S_x, S_y, N, dt
Outputs: filter_mean, filter_cov, S_xoS_x_inv, S_yoS_y
Pairs: aci_filter
Note: The vector CGNS filter on a three-dimensional observed and
 three-dimensional unobserved state. Two things are worth a reviewer's eye.
 .
 The observation-noise Grammian is inverted with pinv() -- a pseudoinverse,
 chosen by the authors "for stability concerns" -- where aciR uses a Cholesky
 inverse and its validator requires positive-definiteness at every step. On a
 singular slice the reference returns a Moore-Penrose inverse and continues;
 aciR refuses. aciR is the stricter of the two, and the difference is real
 rather than cosmetic: sigma_I vanishes at the boundaries of I.
 .
 Line 1231 states outright that noise cross-interaction terms are absent from
 this model and the associated matrix functionals are therefore not defined in
 the script. That is the upstream confirmation, pinned to a line, that the
 matrix cross-noise path has no counterpart to be graded against.
 .
 The unobserved paths u, h_W and tau enter only as the initial mean at index 1.
 The initial covariance is the literal 0.1*eye(3), as in the dyad.

Function: ref_enso_smoother
Script: ENSO_model_cond_ACI_u_h_W_tau_unobs.m
Lines: 1278-1338
Inputs: T_C, T_E, I, filter_mean, filter_cov, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, S_yoS_y,
 S_xoS_x_inv, N, dt
Outputs: smoother_mean, smoother_cov, E_j_matrices, F_j_matrices
Pairs: aci_smoother, .aci_online_aux
Note: Backward vector smoother, again computing the online-smoother
 auxiliaries in the same loop.

Function: ref_enso_metric
Script: ENSO_model_cond_ACI_u_h_W_tau_unobs.m
Lines: 1524-1533
Inputs: filter_mean_s, filter_cov_s, smoother_mean_s, smoother_cov_s
Outputs: signal_smoother_filter, dispersion_smoother_filter, ACI_metric
Pairs: aci_metric
Note: The multivariate relative entropy, formed with det() and a right matrix
 division where aciR uses a Cholesky factorisation -- algebraically the same
 quantity, numerically a different route, so these two agree to round-off
 rather than exactly. Operates on the windowed `_s` slices rather than the full
 record.

